1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:30,409
LitigatAI, an AI workbench for Indian litigation
Eleven tools over one document set: chronology, judgment briefs, authority checks, cited research, drafting and a hearing diary. FastAPI, a no build front end, and free to host.

2
00:00:30,409 --> 00:00:51,429
Bring your own API key
The deployment need hold no key at all. A visitor pastes their own, it is tested against the provider before being accepted, kept in their browser, and used only for their own requests.

3
00:00:51,429 --> 00:01:10,351
Four providers, live model lists, switched on the spot
Gemini, Groq, OpenAI and Hugging Face. Each is asked what it can actually serve, so a retired model drops out on its own. Switching to Groq here, and every tool that follows runs on it.

4
00:01:10,351 --> 00:01:37,522
List of Dates, from a real PDF brief
A works contract dispute uploaded as a PDF. PyMuPDF pulls the text server side, then the model returns a filing ready chronology with every entry tied back to the document.

5
00:01:37,522 --> 00:02:01,371
Judgment Summariser
A Supreme Court arbitration judgment in, a structured brief out: facts, issues, what was held, the ratio and why it matters. Argue from this rather than re-reading forty paragraphs.

6
00:02:01,371 --> 00:02:23,923
Authority Check
Before you cite it, find out whether it is still good law. The tool reports whether a judgment has been followed, distinguished, doubted or overruled, and by whom.

7
00:02:23,923 --> 00:02:45,378
Translate and OCR
A consumer complaint filed in Hindi, uploaded as a DOCX. The language is detected and the document is translated into English with the statutory references and the figures intact. A scanned page goes to a vision model.

8
00:02:45,378 --> 00:03:10,128
Legal Research
An Indian law question answered with citations, and with live Indian Kanoon retrieval when a token is configured. The answer names the sections and judgments it relies on.

9
00:03:10,128 --> 00:03:33,164
Prompt Enhancer
A rough question becomes a structured research instruction: the issues named, the statutes to check listed, the output format specified. Better prompt, better answer, and it hands straight to research.

10
00:03:33,164 --> 00:03:56,061
Bare Acts, browse and amendments
Eleven statutes indexed section by section, including the Consumer Protection Act 2019 and the new criminal codes, each section carrying the amendments that changed it. This data ships with the repository.

11
00:03:56,061 --> 00:04:24,056
Bare Acts, question a provision
Pick an Act and a section, then ask about it. The section text is quoted back and the answer is confined to that provision, which keeps it grounded rather than drifting into general commentary.

12
00:04:24,056 --> 00:04:58,065
Document Drafter
Brief the drafter on the matter and it produces a court ready pleading with citations marked inline, exportable as DOCX or PDF. Nine document types, from a plaint to an arbitration statement of claim.

13
00:04:58,065 --> 00:05:27,311
Counter Arguments
Upload the other side's written statement. Every distinct claim is pulled out so each can be answered on its own, then rebutted and assembled into a written statement.

14
00:05:27,311 --> 00:05:59,126
Court Tracker
Every matter with its next date in one diary, an overdue flag, hearing outcomes logged per case, CSV export and an eCourts CNR lookup. Seeded here with five sample matters so a fresh deployment is never a blank screen.

15
00:05:59,126 --> 00:06:17,944
Free to host, and nobody pays for anyone else
One FastAPI service inside 512 MB, a statute index committed to the repository, seeded demo data, and every provider key supplied by whoever is using it. That is the whole hosting bill.
